{"id":"refine_20260929131226388","summary":"Create a global memory for task-1c897e72’s demonstrated 3×3 tile-compression rule.","rationale":"The demos and solved test consistently map each aligned 3×3 input tile to one output cell, preserving any marker color in that tile and outputting 0 when the tile contains only 0 and 5; the solved test confirms markers are not reduced to component anchors.","expectedOutcome":"Future instances of task-1c897e72 should produce an H/3 by W/3 grid by checking each non-overlapping 3×3 tile independently; validate dimensions, tile alignment, and marker preservation against examples.","appliedEdits":[{"action":"create","kind":"memory","id":"arc_task_1c897e72_compress_3x3_tiles_by_marker_presence","title":"ARC task-1c897e72: compress aligned 3×3 tiles by marker presence","content":"For recurring ARC task task-1c897e72, demonstrations (24×24→8×8, 21×21→7×7, 27×27→9×9) and a solved 9×9→3×3 test establish independent 3×3 tile compression. Partition the input into non-overlapping 3×3 tiles aligned from the top-left; output dimensions are H/3 by W/3. For each tile, output 0 if all cells are background colors 0 or 5. Otherwise output the non-background marker color present in that tile (demonstrated marker colors include 3, 4, 6, 7, 8, and 9). Preserve sparse placement and evaluate each tile independently: the same broad or connected marker region may occupy multiple output cells; do not collapse components to anchors. A solved example yields [[8,0,8],[0,8,0],[0,3,3]]. Multiple marker colors in one tile were not evidenced, so do not assume a tie rule.","metadata":{"scope":"global"},"reason":"Persists the demonstrated recurring task rule and the important independent-tile versus connected-component distinction.","after":{"id":"arc_task_1c897e72_compress_3x3_tiles_by_marker_presence","kind":"memory","title":"ARC task-1c897e72: compress aligned 3×3 tiles by marker presence","content":"For recurring ARC task task-1c897e72, demonstrations (24×24→8