{"id":"refine_20260929132954661","summary":"Created a global memory for the demonstrated diagonal-ray behavior in ARC task-f17c4ceb, with a caution to verify which diagonal pairs qualify.","rationale":"The test was solved correctly by extending a diagonal run of two 2s backward through background cells and stopping before a different-colored cell. Demo 1 also shows several extended diagonal runs, but Demo 2 contains a diagonal pair that remains unchanged, so the rule needs task-specific qualification rather than unconditional application.","expectedOutcome":"Future instances of task-f17c4ceb can recognize demonstrated diagonal extensions while checking pair eligibility, ray direction, blockers, and preservation of the original grid. Validate proposed extensions against all available demonstrations, especially the unchanged configuration.","appliedEdits":[{"action":"create","kind":"memory","id":"arc_task_f17c4ceb_extend_qualifying_diagonal_runs","title":"ARC task-f17c4ceb: extend demonstrated qualifying diagonal runs","content":"For recurring ARC task-f17c4ceb, demonstrations and a correct test submission support extending certain adjacent same-color diagonal runs along their diagonal through background cells, preserving dimensions and original cells. Stop extension at the grid boundary or before a non-background cell. In the solved test, background was 8; 2s at (r6,c3) and (r7,c4) defined a down-right diagonal. Extend backward with 2s at (r5,c2), (r4,c1), and (r3,c0); the forward ray stopped before the 6 at (r8,c5). A separate vertical adjacency of 2s did not define another ray. Important qualification: Demo 2 contains a diagonal 7 pair in a small 2×2 colored arrangement, yet its output is unchanged. Therefore do not extend every same-color diagonal pair automatically; check the surrounding configuration against demonstrations and leave nonqualifying arrangements unchanged.","metadata":{"scope":"global","task_id":"task-f17c4ceb"},"reason":"The transformation yielded