umn 6 and a full horizontal divider at row 8, with other cells of that color left unchanged. A correctly solved test had full dividers at columns 1 and 6 and rows 12 and 22; those lines changed from color 3 to 2 while all other cells remained unchanged. Verify candidate lines span the entire grid before recoloring.","path":"general","scope":"global","reference":{},"arguments":{},"metadata":{"scope":"global"},"source":"refine","created_at":"2026-09-29T14:01:32.888Z","updated_at":"2026-09-29T14:01:32.888Z","version":1},"applied":true}],"harnessStatePath":"/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-primeagentlearner-1790682248/attempts/6d3823d65d4947dd91c6d88a902b3f7d/system/state/agent/harness/harness_state.json","scope":"global"}
{"id":"refine_20260929140437303","summary":"Updated the global task-d52d454b memory with verified diagonal-period completion evidence and a warning against leaving sparse inputs unchanged.","rationale":"The task was solved by grouping known cells by diagonal offset, inferring the shortest consistent period, and extending its color mapping across the grid; other demonstrations show row-period completion as well.","expectedOutcome":"Future solvers should recognize diagonal or horizontal periodic structure, infer the shortest period consistent with all clues, and validate the completed grid against every known cell.","appliedEdits":[{"action":"update","kind":"memory","id":"arc_task_d52d454b_reconstruct_sparse_periodic_grid","title":"ARC task-d52d454b: reconstruct periodic grids from sparse colored clues","content":"For recurring ARC task-d52d454b, zeros can be missing/background placeholders in a periodic color pattern. Determine the pattern orientation from the clues. For diagonal stripes, group nonzero cells by offset d = column − row, infer the shortest period p consistent with all known colors, then map each residue d mod p to its color and fill every cell accordingly. Verified 14×14 demo: diagonal period 4 produced rows cycling [8,5,4,1], [1,8,5,4], [4,1,8,5], [5,4,1,8]. Verified solved 8×8 test: offsets d=3 had color 6, d=−1 color 9, d=−4 color 3, d=−5 color 1, and d=7 color 5; shortest consistent period was 5, with residues (column−row mod 5): 0→1, 1→3, 2→5, 3→6, 4→9. This completion was correct. Other demos show horizontal full-row colors, where infer the shortest vertical period of row colors and extend it through blank rows. Do not leave a sparse diagonal test unchanged; check candidate periods against every clue before filling.","metadata":{"scope":"global"},"reason":"Adds a verified diagonal-period rule and solved color mapping to the existing task-specific memory while retaining the demonstrated row-period case.","before":{"id":"arc_task_d52d454b_reconstruct_sparse_periodic_grid","kind":"memory","title":"ARC task-d52d454b: reconstruct sparse periodic grids from colored clues","content":"For recurring task-d52d454b, sparse nonzero cells can encode a periodic color pattern; zeros may be missing/background placeholders rather than output zeros. Identify the invariant coordinate for the observed parallel stripes: row index for horizontal stripes, c-r for positive-slope diagonals, or r+c for negative-slope diagonals. Infer the shortest period and color assignment consistent with every clue, then fill the full grid by repeating it and verify all marked cells. Demonstrations include full-width row stripes with missing zero rows, and a 28×28 diagonal pattern repeating by c-r mod 4 with rows [9,5,8,3], [3,9,5,8], [8,3,9,5], [5,8,3,9]. In the solved 15×15 instance, markers established (r+c) mod 3 mapping 0→4, 1→2, 2→1. Do not assume a fixed period or orientation; infer them from the input clues.","path":"general","scope":"global","reference":{},"arguments":{},"metadata":{"scope":"global"},"source":"refine","created_at":"2026-09-29T13:16:28.316Z","updated_at":"2026-09-29T13:16:28.316Z","version":1},"after":{"id":"arc_task_d52d454b_reconstruct_sparse_periodic_grid","kind":"memory","title":"ARC task-d52d454b: reconstruct periodic grids from sparse colored clues","content":"For recurring ARC task-d52d454b, zeros can be missing/background placeholders in a periodic color pattern. Determine the pattern orientation from the clues. For diagonal stripes, group nonzero cells by offset d = column − row, infer the shortest period p consistent with all known colors, then map each residue d mod p to its color and fill every cell accordingly. Verified 14×14 demo: diagonal period 4 produced rows cycling [8,5,4,1], [1,8,5,4], [4,1,8,5], [5,4,1,8]. Verified solved 8×8 test: offsets d=3 had color 6, d=−1 color 9, d=−4 color 3, d=−5 color 1, and d=7 color 5; shortest consistent period was 5, with residues (column−row mod 5): 0→1, 1→3, 2→5, 3→6, 4→9. This completion was correct. Other demos show horizontal full-row colors, where infer the shortest vertical period of row colors and extend it through blank rows. Do not leave a sparse diagonal test unchanged; check candidate periods against every clue before filling.","path":"general","scope":"global","reference":{},"arguments":{},"metadata":{"scope":"global"},"source":"refine","created_at":"2026-09-29T13:16:28.316Z","updated_at":"2026-09-29T14:04:44.886Z","version":2},"applied":true}],"harnessStatePath":"/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-primeagentlearner-1790682248/attempts/6d3823d65d4947dd91c6d88a902b3f7d/system/state/agent/harness/harness_state.json","scope":"global"}
{"id":"refine_20260929140545799","summary":"Updated the task-b5ac2009 memory with the verified trailing-fill behavior and its contextual limits.","rationale":"The global harness already contains a task-b5ac2009 memory cautioning that not every trailing background run should be filled. These clean demonstrations add a confirmed case: partial left-aligned horizontal runs are extended with color 1, while full-width runs and unrelated rows remain unchanged.","expectedOutcome":"Future solvers can apply this demonstrated rule when the task structure matches, while retaining the warning to use context rather than filling every partial run indiscriminately.","appliedEdits":[{"action":"update","kind":"memory","id":"arc_task_b5ac2009_complete_trailing_background_after_left_aligned_runs","title":"ARC task-b5ac2009: context-dependent trailing-background markers (verified examples and caution)","content":"For recurring ARC task-b5ac2009, demonstrations support replacing selected trailing background cells after partial horizontal runs with color 1; do not assume every trailing background after any foreground run should be filled. In clean examples, a full-width horizontal line of a foreground color coexists with one or more partial horizontal runs of that same color beginning at the left edge. Extend each demonstrated partial run to the right edge using color 1, preserve its original foreground cells, leave the already-full line unchanged, and preserve unrelated rows. Verified examples: a 27×16 grid had row 5 (0-based) color 4 in columns 0–7, extended with 1 in columns 8–15, while the full row 18 remained unchanged; a 26×9 grid had partial color-2 runs in rows 23 (columns 0–2) and 25 (column 2), each extended through the remaining columns with 1, while full row 10 remained unchanged. A 16×16 test had a full color-2 row 3 and a partial run in row 12, columns 0–10; extending row 12, columns 11–15 with 1 was correct. Dimensions and colors may vary. Validate that the run and surrounding structure match the demonstrations before filling.","metadata":{"scope":"global"},"reason":"Incorporates the requested verified examples into the existing task-specific memory without discarding its established caution against overgeneralizing.","before":{"id":"arc_task_b5ac2009_complete_trailing_background_after_left_aligned_runs","kind":"memory","title":"ARC task-b5ac2009: context-dependent trailing-background markers (verified examples and caution)","content":"Recurring ARC task-b5ac2009 (colors vary): color 1 is used to replace selected trailing background cells at row ends, but “fill every trailing background after any foreground run” is NOT a reliable rule. Demonstrations show strongly context-dependent row selection; preserve all other cells and dimensions. Obtain/compare demos before applying a broad suffix rule.\n\nObserved demo evidence (row numbers 1-based; outputs):\n- 10x10: row 3 is 7,7,7,7 followed by six 6s; output changes those six trailing cells to 1. A full-width row of 7s remains unchanged.\n- 10x18: row 9 has seventeen 2s then a final 4; only that last cell becomes 1. A full-width 2 row remains unchanged.\n- 10x10: row 7 has three 7s followed by seven 0s; output changes that suffix to 1. Other full-width 7 rows remain unchanged.\n- 20x23: lower group rows 16-17 each have trailing two 8s changed to 1; row 18 has four trailing 8s and is unchanged. This warns that suffix length/pattern context matters.\n- 27x17: in the lower pattern, rows 22, 24, 25, 26 have the final four background 3s replaced by 1; row 23 (a 10-cell leading 7 run then background) and row 27 are unchanged.\n- 27x26: row 23 has nine leading 7s then zeros to the edge; all trailing zeros become 1. Other rows unchanged.\n- 10x10: row 9 ends with two 7s after a mixed 2/7 pattern; those final two cells become 1. Other rows unchanged.\n- 27x10: rows 5 and 7 end with two 4s after mixed 8/4 patterns; those final two cells become 1. Other rows unchanged.\n\nThe previous compact memory (“partial left-aligned runs: recolor trailing background”) is too broad. On a 29x24 test with color 5 background and 7 foreground, eight attempted variants all failed, including: changing only the final 5 after the row starting with 7; changing the immediate gap after that leading 7; recoloring all 5s after the first 7; recoloring right-end background suffixes in the two lower patterned rows; combinations of those; and no-op. Do not assume every visibly partial suffix qualifies. In future instances, request demos and look for row/group context that selects qualifying lines, including similarities or differences between repeated pattern blocks. Submission outputs above are confirmed; exact selection rule remains unresolved.","path":"general","scope":"global","reference":{},"arguments":{},"metadata":{"scope":"global"},"source":"agent","created_at":"2026-09-29T13:29:11.233Z","updated_at":"2026-09-29T13:35:23.593086+00:00","version":2},"after":{"id":"arc_task_b5ac2009_complete_trailing_background_after_left_aligned_runs","kind":"memory","title":"ARC task-b5ac2009: context-dependent trailing-background markers (verified examples and caution)","content":"For recurring ARC task-b5ac2009, demonstrations support replacing selected trailing background cells after partial horizontal runs with color 1; do not assume every trailing background after any foreground run should be filled. In clean examples, a full-width horizontal line of a foreground color coexists with one or more partial horizontal runs of that same color beginning at the left edge. Extend each demonstrated partial run to the right edge using color 1, preserve its original foreground cells, leave the already-full line unchanged, and preserve unrelated rows. Verified examples: a 27×16 grid had row 5 (0-based) color 4 in columns 0–7, extended with 1 in columns 8–15, while the full row 18 remained unchanged; a 26×9 grid had partial color-2 runs in rows 23 (columns 0–2) and 25 (column 2), each extended through the re